---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Dithering studio" description="Turn any picture into 1-bit pixels, halftone dots or symbols in your browser. Floyd–Steinberg, Atkinson, Stucki, Bayer, blue noise. Export PNG or SVG." wide>
  <h1>Dithering studio</h1>
  <p class="lead">Two colours, and the illusion of every grey in between.</p>
  <p>Dithering decides, pixel by pixel, ink or paper, so that from a step back the average looks right. Error diffusion (Floyd–Steinberg and friends) pushes each pixel's rounding error onto its neighbours. Ordered dithering compares each pixel with a fixed pattern: Bayer's matrix gives the classic crosshatch, blue noise looks like fine grain. Your picture stays in your browser.</p>

  <div class="tool">
    <div class="panel controls" id="controls" data-frame>
      <div class="drop" id="drop" data-sample="/media/sample-voron.jpg">
        <label for="file"><strong>Choose a picture</strong>, drop it here, or paste it.</label>
        <input type="file" id="file" accept="image/*" class="sr">
        <span class="note">It starts with my Voron printer.</span>
      </div>

      <fieldset>
        <legend>Look</legend>
        <label>Render as
          <select id="mode">
            <option value="pixels">1-bit pixels</option>
            <option value="dots">halftone dots</option>
            <option value="symbols">symbols</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label data-for-mode="pixels">Algorithm
          <select id="algo">
            <optgroup label="Error diffusion">
              <option value="floyd-steinberg">Floyd–Steinberg</option>
              <option value="atkinson" selected>Atkinson</option>
              <option value="jarvis-judice-ninke">Jarvis, Judice, Ninke</option>
              <option value="stucki">Stucki</option>
              <option value="burkes">Burkes</option>
              <option value="sierra">Sierra</option>
              <option value="sierra-lite">Sierra Lite</option>
            </optgroup>
            <optgroup label="Ordered">
              <option value="bayer2">Bayer 2×2</option>
              <option value="bayer4">Bayer 4×4</option>
              <option value="bayer8">Bayer 8×8</option>
              <option value="bayer16">Bayer 16×16</option>
              <option value="blue">Blue noise (void and cluster)</option>
            </optgroup>
            <option value="threshold">Plain threshold</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label data-for-mode="pixels"><input type="checkbox" id="serp" checked> Serpentine scan (error diffusion)</label>
        <label>Width <span class="val" data-val="width"></span>
          <input type="range" id="width" min="40" max="1200" step="10" value="420">
        </label>
        <label data-for-mode="dots symbols">Cell size <span class="val" data-val="cell"></span>
          <input type="range" id="cell" min="4" max="24" step="1" value="8">
        </label>
        <label data-for-mode="symbols">Symbols, light to dark
          <input type="text" id="chars" value=" .:-=+*#%@" spellcheck="false">
        </label>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset>
        <legend>Tone</legend>
        <label>Brightness <span class="val" data-val="bright"></span>
          <input type="range" id="bright" min="-0.5" max="0.5" step="0.01" value="0">
        </label>
        <label>Contrast <span class="val" data-val="contrast"></span>
          <input type="range" id="contrast" min="0.2" max="3" step="0.05" value="1.1">
        </label>
        <label>Gamma <span class="val" data-val="gamma"></span>
          <input type="range" id="gamma" min="0.3" max="3" step="0.05" value="1">
        </label>
        <label data-for-mode="pixels">Threshold <span class="val" data-val="thresh"></span>
          <input type="range" id="thresh" min="0.1" max="0.9" step="0.01" value="0.5">
        </label>
        <label><input type="checkbox" id="invert"> Invert</label>
        <div class="row">
          <label>Ink <input type="color" id="ink" value="#000000"></label>
          <label>Paper <input type="color" id="paper" value="#ffffff"></label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset>
        <legend>Export</legend>
        <label>PNG scale <span class="val" data-val="scale"></span>
          <input type="range" id="scale" min="1" max="8" step="1" value="1">
        </label>
        <div class="row">
          <button class="btn" type="button" id="png" data-frame disabled>Save PNG</button>
          <button class="btn" type="button" id="svg" data-frame disabled>Save SVG</button>
        </div>
        <span class="note">SVG draws pixel runs as one path, dots as circles and symbols as text: ready for a pen plotter or a laser cutter.</span>
      </fieldset>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <canvas id="out" role="img" aria-label="The dithered picture"></canvas>
      <p class="note" id="status" aria-live="polite">Loading…</p>
      <label class="note"><input type="checkbox" id="orig"> Show the original below</label>
      <img id="original" alt="The original picture" hidden style="margin-top:10px;max-width:100%">
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 data-under>How the algorithms differ</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Floyd–Steinberg</strong> (1976) spreads the error to four neighbours with weights 7, 3, 5 and 1 sixteenths. Faithful tones, a little wormy in flat areas.</li>
    <li><strong>Atkinson</strong>, from the original Macintosh, passes on only 6/8 of the error. Highlights and shadows clip, which gives that crisp, high-contrast look.</li>
    <li><strong>Jarvis–Judice–Ninke, Stucki, Burkes, Sierra</strong> spread the error over larger neighbourhoods: smoother, softer, slower.</li>
    <li><strong>Bayer</strong> compares each pixel with a recursive threshold matrix. Regular crosshatch; easy to compute in parallel, which is why old games used it.</li>
    <li><strong>Blue noise</strong> uses a 64×64 threshold pattern built here in your browser with Ulichney's void-and-cluster method. The points avoid each other at every scale, so it reads as fine, even grain.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
<script>
  import '../../scripts/tools/dither';
</script>
